\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Operators and coordinate realizations}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Definitions and mathematical statements specify their modules or spaces,
maps and forms. Products of composable maps apply the right factor first,
as in [composition and field multiplication](fdip-0011). A
[component for a specified splitting](fdip-001T) is a composite
with its inclusion and projection. Site evaluation, an
oriented edge and a character of a specified finite group are part of the
given data; their labels do not require an additional choice of basis.}

\p{A proof that uses coordinates first names the basis or the isomorphism
to a coordinate space. Matrix realizations and their entries are otherwise
given in Examples or Remarks, together with that identification. A statement
that a basis with a specified property exists is a statement about the space,
not a fixed coordinate realization. Explicit realizations retain their
calculations and the connection to the operators they represent.}

\p{A form and an endomorphism have different types. The
[dual module](ca-0012) records linear functionals; identifying it with
the original module requires specified duality data, as in
the [chosen perfect pairing](fdip-001O). The
[adjoint for that pairing](fdip-001P) uses a chosen symmetric
bilinear pairing. A Hermitian adjoint instead uses the stated scalar
conjugation and Hermitian form. Positivity, normalization and topology are
assumed only where the individual statement specifies them.}
